1
00:00:03,401 --> 00:00:10,683
Deep dive: pricing. What the app prices, how it splits a sheet’s cost, and how to build a full quote from its numbers. Example prices throughout.

2
00:00:10,683 --> 00:00:20,859
Prices live on each material in the Library. Open the white melamine.

3
00:00:20,859 --> 00:00:34,537
Costed by area, $2.85 per square foot. A 5 x 12 ft sheet is 60 ft², so one sheet costs $171. Price per ft² keeps working when the sheet size changes.

4
00:00:34,537 --> 00:00:43,450
Density is only a label; nothing here touches cut sizes. Cancel. The other boards were priced the same way in Real jobs u06.

5
00:00:43,450 --> 00:01:03,130
Back in the job, Nesting. Area and cost splits every sheet into three: the parts, remnants worth keeping, and scrap.

6
00:01:03,130 --> 00:01:16,515
Sheet 1, the 1/4" HDF backs: $51.01 a sheet. The 13 parts use 81.8% of it ($41.71); the rest is scrap ($9.30). You pay for the whole sheet either way.

7
00:01:16,515 --> 00:01:31,493
Sheet 4, melamine, 93% used: the better the nest, the less of each sheet is scrap. That is why spacing and rotation settings matter to price.

8
00:01:31,493 --> 00:01:47,241
Sheet 6 leaves big offcuts. Remnants kept have their own line: material you still own, and can use on the next job.

9
00:01:47,241 --> 00:02:01,921
The job line: six sheets cost $825.09. Less the remnants you keep, the job uses $625.75. Quote the first to be safe, the second to be sharp.

10
00:02:01,921 --> 00:02:12,061
The Quote tab turns those numbers into a quote. This job already has the EXAMPLE prices typed in Advanced examples e06.

11
00:02:12,061 --> 00:02:25,821
Board is the whole sheets bought, $825.10, not the $625.75 after remnants: you pay the supplier for whole sheets. Credit remnants yourself if you reuse them.

12
00:02:25,821 --> 00:02:38,517
Why $825.10 and not $825.09? The quote rounds each item to the cent, then adds the lines, so the PDF’s lines add up exactly.

13
00:02:38,517 --> 00:02:49,960
Edgeband uses the BOM’s running feet, which include the 2" trimming allowance per edge: you buy the waste too.

14
00:02:49,960 --> 00:03:00,735
Hardware is per BOM unit: a hinge, a plate, and one TANDEM runner set per drawer. Price it the way your supplier sells it.

15
00:03:00,735 --> 00:03:12,807
Labour is time per cabinet, drawer, door and sheet cut, at your shop rate. Simple on purpose: the times are yours, and start as badged placeholders.

16
00:03:12,807 --> 00:03:26,577
Markup is on the subtotal; sales tax on subtotal + markup. Both are per job, because jobs differ (a contractor, a tax-exempt client).

17
00:03:26,577 --> 00:03:36,022
What if a price goes missing? Clear the hinge’s example price in the Library…

18
00:03:36,022 --> 00:04:01,337
…and the quote says so: "price not set for 1 of 6 items", and the total is marked incomplete. Never a silent $0 that undercuts you.

19
00:04:01,337 --> 00:04:25,918
Put the example price back ($4.50) and the total is whole again.

20
00:04:25,918 --> 00:04:51,300
Per part: the Areas and costs CSV gives each part its share of its sheet, and now ends with the same quote block. Useful for a change order.

21
00:04:51,300 --> 00:04:59,105
So: price boards per ft² and nest well, decide how you treat remnants, set edgeband, hardware and labour once, and let "price not set" catch the gaps.
